<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yasser Arafat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1929">1929</a>–<a href="/years/2004">2004</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Abu Ammar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yasser Arafat was the Palestinian nationalist leader who chaired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine-liberation-organization">Palestine Liberation Organization</a> from 1969 until his death and became the face of the Palestinian cause. A founder of Fatah in 1959, he pursued armed struggle and later negotiation, addressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1974, signed the Oslo Accords with Israel in 1993, shared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1994 and led the new Palestinian Authority from Ramallah. He died near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 2004 with Palestinian statehood unresolved.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yasser-arafat.jpg" alt="Yasser Arafat" width="330" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gideon Markowiz · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leader_of_the_PLO,_Yasser_Arafat,_1996_Dan_Hadani_Archive.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Arafat was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> on 4 or 24 August 1929, the second-youngest of seven children of Palestinian parents. His father, Abdel Raouf al-Qudwa al-Husseini, was from Gaza City and worked as a textile merchant in Cairo. His mother, Zahwa Abul Saud, whose family home was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, died in 1933. He enrolled at the University of King Fuad I in 1944 and graduated in 1950, and by 1946 was an Arab nationalist procuring weapons to be smuggled into Mandatory Palestine. When the 1948 Arab-Israeli War began he left the university and fought in the Gaza area alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-brotherhood">Muslim Brotherhood</a>, without joining it. He later served as president of the General Union of Palestinian Students from 1952 to 1956.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a> of 1956 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> forces in Gaza were expelled, Arafat among them. In 1957 he obtained a visa for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwait">Kuwait</a>, where he met Salah Khalaf and Khalil al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wazir">Wazir</a>, who became his top aides. In the late 1950s he co-founded Fatah, a paramilitary organisation seeking Israel's replacement with a Palestinian state, which he led from 1959. He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine-liberation-organization">Palestine Liberation Organization</a> in 1967 and in 1969 was elected chair of the Palestinian National Council. Fatah's presence in Jordan brought clashes with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-of-jordan">King Hussein</a>'s government, and in the early 1970s it moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, where it took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanese-civil-war">Lebanese Civil War</a> and became a target of Israeli invasions in 1978 and 1982.</p><p>From 1983 to 1993 he was based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, and in 1985 survived an Israeli bombing of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> headquarters in which 73 people died. He shifted from open conflict to negotiation, acknowledging Israel's right to exist in 1988, and took part in the Madrid Conference of 1991, the talks that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo-accords">Oslo Accords</a> of 1993 and the Camp David Summit of 2000. He shared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1994 with Yitzhak Rabin and Shimon Peres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After being confined in his Ramallah compound by the Israeli army for over two years, Arafat fell into a coma in late 2004 and died on 11 November 2004; the cause of his death remains the subject of speculation. Palestinians generally view him as a martyr who symbolised their national aspirations, while many Israelis regarded him as a terrorist. Palestinian rivals often denounced him as corrupt or too submissive in his concessions to Israel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yasser Arafat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Israel and the PLO signed the Declaration of Principles at the White House, with a historic handshake between Yitzhak Rabin and <b>Yasser Arafat</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-13">13 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Palestine Liberation Organization, founded in 1964 and led from 1969 by <b>Yasser Arafat</b>, came to stand for Palestinian nationhood after the Nakba of 1948 had scattered much of the Arab population of Mandatory Palestine.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-palestine">State of Palestine</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in Hôpital d'instruction des armées Percy, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1977" aria-label="Cairo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Yasser Arafat"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5091 4996h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5091 4996h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1977">What was here in 1977? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
